    "proof": [
      "Under z, O is a grid and isolates the other central line in the side-component graph. Contract the geometric mate/central-flip edges. The remaining five vertices are the exterior mate pairs, cyclically permuted by z. Their invariant component partition has blocks of equal size dividing5: either five singleton components or one component. Thus each c is2 or6, already under z alone.",
      "The hM both-grid obstruction excludes(6,6) under T/U PSD, so min(c_l,c_m)=2 for every member of this family. This is structural, not merely a property of the producer sample.",
      "At total nullity9, nu0=5 and a+b=1. The four star kernels give g-parities(2,2); the O indicator adds one positive direction. The remaining four directions form one Q(zeta5)-line, on which the commuting involution g acts by+1 or-1. Hence profiles(7,2) or(3,6). In c22 these are precisely the four mixed relations; in c62/c26 the four grid relations are positive.",
      "Write e,f=+1 for the matching R between mate-central stars of type D_s, and -1 for R=M. This R is a different matching from the matching within a star. Write t,w for the signed cell-counts of B,C. Evaluating the unreduced Laurent representatives of T_- at X=1 gives a matrix orthogonally similar to3I2 plus P=[[7/2-e,-t-w-1/2],[-t-w-1/2,7/2-f]]. Evaluation at1 is not a map Q(zeta5)->Q; only its residue modulo5 is intrinsic.",
      "For N=4T_-, det N at1 is576*d, d=(7-2e)(7-2f)-(2(t+w)+1)^2. The map Z[zeta5]->F5 sending zeta to1 is well-defined because Phi5(1)=5. If d is nonzero modulo5, det N is nonzero in Q(zeta5): the negative nontrivial kernel is absent. No converse is asserted.",
      "Seven of the14 quotient types have this unit residue. In all seven nu0=5; combining b=0 with the proved a<=1 gives local nullity<=9, without ODD9 or the global modular rank bound.",
      "At nullity9 the negative profile(3,6) survives this necessary filter only for five quotient types: one e is+1 and the other-1 with(t,w)=(2,0) or(0,2), or e=f=-1,t=w=0. Thus it requires R=M on at least one side. These are possible quotient types, not asserted existing graphs.",
      "The other two quotient types have nu0=6 and cannot have total nullity9 by the modulo4 formula. Their local existence and a structural ceiling9 for the remaining types are not settled by this calculation."
    ]
  },
  "proof": [
    "C1 makes the four z-fixed labels O a C4. C1 then forbids every O-to-outer edge. Each outer star matching is even_i--odd_(i+a); the mate-star matching is either D_s=diag(z^s,z^-s), or M. In the latter case a=0 violates C2.",
    "On each nontrivial z-character, split by g=hM into4x4 blocks. T+=3I-Gamma+-J/2, T-=3I-Gamma--ff^t/4, f=(1,-1,1,-1). Gamma cross-blocks are B+C*M and B-C*M. Each B,C is binary circulant with row and column sums2.",
    "The z-trivial space has dimension12. After C1 its variable tests reduce to T on M-: [[7/2-e,-t-w-1/2],[-t-w-1/2,7/2-f]] and [[4+e,-t+w],[-t+w,4+f]]. Direct exact12x12 T/U quotient tests leave14 cases, nullities5 or6. Nullity6 leaves only e=f=1, (t,w)=(2,0) or(0,2).",
    "Over Q(zeta5), left principal2x2 blocks of T+ are positive definite unless D,a=s=0: triangle inequality on 1+2z^a+2z^s (diagonal5) or 1+2z^a (diagonal3). T- blocks are always positive definite: off-diagonal modulus<=9<11 or<=5<15. Thus rank<=2 forces the entire2x2 Schur complement to vanish.",
    "Each T+ cross column uses two coefficient vectors in{0,1,2}^5 with total4. Each T- cross column uses at most2 positive and2 negative coefficients across10 positions. These finite pools cover every binary B,C. The code first tests both diagonal Schur equations, then its off-diagonal equation, exactly in Z[zeta5].",
    "For T+,128 possible first columns and128 second columns give304 rank2 blocks;3152 binary lifts,520 admissible. Every admissible lift has an explicit integer q with q^t(8T)q<0 or q^t(15U)q<0. For T-,32 first and32 second columns give no rank2 block. Hence both field nullities a,b are<=1.",
    "If just one T+ diagonal block is singular, conjugate by tau=(02)(13). It commutes with z,hM, preserves the mate geometry and swaps the two lines. The internal parameters swap; B becomes B^* and C becomes (M*C*M)^*, still binary circulants with the same row/column counts. The nonsingular block is then on the left, so the finite enumeration covers it. If both are singular, both sides contain all5 exterior grids. T-kernel indicators force zero edges between corresponding grids and a perfect matching between distinct grids. Since hM acts antipodally on each C4, those matchings are C4 automorphisms. On normalized checkerboards U is [[2I5,B],[B^t,2I5]], B diagonal0, otherwise+/-1. PSD implies BB^t=4I; two rows overlap in3 positions, so their inner product is odd: impossible. This last argument does not require z.",
    "Galois conjugacy gives null_Q T=nu0+4a+4b<=14. A Conway completion needs nullity>=14, hence nu0=6,a=b=1. Swapping2,3 normalizes the remaining quotient to B having diagonal cell-counts2 and off-diagonal0, C all cell-counts1. This swap commutes with z and hM.",
    "In that quotient the z-trivial rank overF7 is5. Phi5 is irreducible overF7 since7 has order4 modulo5. The rational character splitting remains invertible modulo7: group order10 and denominators2,4,5,8,15 are units.",
    "The same necessary Schur equations modulo7 have no T+ rank<=2 block (88 columns on each side). Its only singular left principal block is the same grid case, covered by line exchange or the both-grid contradiction. For T-,212 columns on each side yield16 blocks. Only8 binary lifts have the normalized cell-counts, and all8 violate C1prime:3 neighbours in a partial outer star whose cap is2. Therefore both field ranks are>=3.",
    "Consequently, whenever local T/U PSD and rational nullity14 hold, rank_F7(8T)>=5+4*3+4*3=29. The accepted earlier GLOBAL bound rank_F7(8T)<=28 rules out extension of every border in this local symmetry family. That global bound is an input from the existing research, not proved anew here.",
    "No claim that all local PSD borders have nullity<=9, that all their relations are side relations, or that H-MATCH/C1 is resolved in general."
  ]
